  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title Manufacture originals — a defect is only visible where one survives

  |summary
    Edits carry their own detector because a diff exists; first writings do not,
    which makes the least-checkable material exactly what corpora are built from.

  |formal-expression
    A defect introduced by editing is detectable, because the pre-edit state exists
    and the two can be compared. A defect present in a first writing has nothing to
    be compared against, so no comparison can find it.

    The consequence is uncomfortable and structural: the least-checkable material in
    any corpus is fresh outlines, new convention files, and syntheses — which is
    most of what agents produce, and precisely what corpora get built out of.

    The move is therefore to *create* the comparison rather than to look harder:
    keep the instruction beside the artifact, the pre-edit text, the brief that
    produced the file, the prediction before the reading.

  |epistemic-status
    **Specimen.** Four of five defects caught in one session were caught by diffing
    against a surviving original — an instruction against the banner that landed; a
    verbatim text against the rewrite of it; a commit's stated intent against its
    one-line repair; an earlier commit's finding against a later change. The fifth
    was caught by the steward, out of knowledge no diff could supply.

    **Specimen, corpus-scale.** The quotation segments in `exempla/` are extracted
    from their primary programmatically and verified back against it. Four separate
    defect classes were found that way in one evening — line-wrapping inside a
    quote, collapsed interior whitespace, a hyphenated word split across lines, and
    a substituted quote character — and none of them would have survived contact
    with proofreading, because none of them changes how the text reads.

    Held at **robust-qualitative**: the mechanism is close to definitional, and the
    frequency claim behind *most of what agents produce* is not measured.

  |discussion
    Several practices in this estate already do this without naming it as one
    mechanism: verbatim steward quotes beside assessments, per-segment audit
    reflections, a text law that acts as a reconstruction detector, and the
    collision property that makes two present-truth claims about one thing capable
    of contradicting each other.

    It is also why `exempla/` keeps accounts append-only with corrections chained
    beneath (FORMAT D5). Chaining manufactures the original that makes the
    correction checkable.

  |working-notes
    Recurrence, worth the tally rather than the shrug: the *same* defect class —
    a nested `"…"` silently rewritten as `'…'` while quoting — occurred three times
    in one evening, in three different segments, by one instance that had already
    been corrected for it twice. Intention did not carry it across twenty minutes.
    `bin/check-corpus` catches it in under a second and has now caught every
    instance. This is #ver-act-not-intention measured on the author of
    #ver-act-not-intention.

    Forward: this corpus now has a committed checker (`bin/check-corpus`), which
    closes the note that stood here. What it cannot check is whether a claim is
    true, whether a status is honest, or whether a practice ever fired — and
    pretending otherwise would make it a proxy wearing verification's clothes. Every
    verification run so far has been ad hoc, and each one found real defects. A
    script that re-locates every quoted span in every segment against its primary
    would convert this principle from a habit into a property of the corpus.
